    seg3Desc: "The user segment encompasses all commercial and civil devices equipped with a GNSS antenna and baseband processor. A critical privacy and architectural fact is that GPS receivers are purely passive: they receive downlink radio transmissions but never transmit data back to space. Your device calculates its position locally on its own silicon without informing any satellite where you are.",

    h2Trilateration: "2. The Mathematical Engine: 3D Trilateration",
    pTrilateration1: "Trilateration is the geometric method of determining an unknown position by measuring distances from known reference points. In GPS navigation, the satellites act as the known reference points, and their coordinates in three-dimensional space are derived from their broadcast navigation message. Because radio waves travel through space at the speed of light (c ≈ 299,792,458 meters per second), the distance from any satellite to your device equals the signal propagation time multiplied by c.",
    pTrilateration2: "If you know your exact distance from Satellite A, you must be located somewhere on the surface of an imaginary sphere centered on that satellite. When you add a second satellite (Satellite B), the intersection of two spheres forms a two-dimensional circle. Introducing a third satellite (Satellite C) narrows the intersection down to just two distinct points in space—one of which is near the Earth's surface and the other is far out in space, easily discarded by the receiver's processor.",
    pTrilateration3: "To resolve clock discrepancies between low-cost quartz receiver crystals and spaceborne atomic clocks, a fourth satellite is required. The receiver treats its internal clock error as a fourth unknown variable, calculating 3D coordinates (X, Y, Z) and receiver clock offset simultaneously. See how satellite physics differs from network routing in our comprehensive <a href='/comparisons/gps-vs-ip-location/'>GPS vs IP location comparison</a>.",

