    workedExampleIntro: "To see the mathematics in action, consider how a receiver calculates distance from a satellite broadcast:",
    workedExampleStep1: "1. Signal Departure: At exactly 12:00:00.000000000 UTC, GPS Satellite PRN-14 transmits a code pulse stamped with its atomic clock time.",
    workedExampleStep2: "2. Signal Arrival: The receiver's antenna picks up the pulse at 12:00:00.067380000 UTC, indicating a transit time of 0.06738 seconds (67.38 milliseconds).",
    workedExampleStep3: "3. Pseudorange Computation: Multiplying transit duration by the speed of light: 0.06738 s × 299,792,458 m/s = 20,200,015.8 meters (approximately 20,200 km).",
    workedExampleStep4: "4. Clock Correction: The receiver compares this result with measurements from three other visible satellites, isolating a 12-nanosecond local receiver clock offset and determining the exact ground coordinates.",

    h2Relativity: "3. Relativistic Time Dilation: Einstein in Your Pocket",
    pRelativity1: "GPS is one of the few everyday consumer technologies that cannot function without accounting for both Special and General Relativity. Without continuous relativistic mathematical adjustments, the calculated positions in navigation apps would drift by approximately 10 kilometers every single day, rendering the entire system useless within hours.",
    pRelativity2: "Two opposing relativistic effects are at play in orbit:",
    relativityBullet1: "Special Relativity (Velocity Effect): Because GPS satellites orbit Earth at roughly 14,000 km/h relative to an observer on the ground, time dilation causes their onboard clocks to tick slightly slower than clocks on Earth—losing roughly 7 microseconds per day.",
    relativityBullet2: "General Relativity (Gravitational Effect): Because the satellites orbit 20,200 km above Earth, they experience a weaker gravitational field than clocks on the ground. According to Einstein's general theory, clocks in weaker gravity tick faster—gaining roughly 45 microseconds per day.",
    pRelativity3: "Combining both effects yields a net daily clock gain of approximately +38 microseconds (+45 µs - 7 µs). Satellite engineers compensate for this before launch by calibrating atomic clock oscillators to tick at 10.22999999543 MHz instead of the nominal 10.23 MHz, perfectly synchronizing them with ground time once in orbit.",

    h2Wgs84: "4. The Coordinate Reference: WGS 84 & Datums",
