        q: "Why does GPS take longer to get a location fix when my phone has been off?",
        a: "When a receiver has been powered off for hours or moved hundreds of kilometers, its stored satellite ephemeris data becomes stale (a 'cold start'). It must download a fresh almanac and ephemeris directly from the satellite signals at 50 bits per second, which takes 30 to 90 seconds unless assisted by A-GPS over cellular data."
      },
      {
        q: "What causes GPS signals to become inaccurate or drop out?",
        a: "GPS operates on ultra-high-frequency radio waves (L1 at 1575.42 MHz and L5 at 1176.45 MHz) that cannot easily penetrate dense concrete, metal, or dense foliage. In urban canyons, signals reflect off skyscrapers (multipath interference), introducing transit delays that degrade accuracy."
      },
